          "question": "What is coronary artery disease and how does it develop over time?",
          "answer": "Coronary artery disease is the build-up of atherosclerotic plaque in the arteries that supply the heart muscle. The source describes plaque as developing over decades. It starts with injury to the artery lining, followed by a buildup of cholesterol-rich material beneath the lining (*Coronary artery disease, General clinical reference*).\n\nThe source describes two ways the disease can cause problems:\n\n- **Gradual narrowing** limits blood flow when the heart needs more oxygen, usually during exertion. This causes predictable chest discomfort called stable angina.\n- **Plaque rupture** can trigger a blood clot that suddenly blocks the artery. This is an acute coronary syndrome, and it can happen at a plaque that was never narrow enough to cause symptoms. So a normal stress test in the past does not rule out a heart attack now.\n\nThe retrieved material does not cover symptoms in detail, risk factors, testing, or treatment. A heart failure summary in the same corpus notes that coronary artery disease is one of the most common causes of heart failure (*Heart failure*). If you have questions about your own heart health, a clinician is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
